Port Guard stops devices inside the network by their MAC address: a
listed device - or every device of one maker, by the first bytes of the
address - can send nothing through the firewall and receive nothing
through it. The page lists the devices the firewall sees, each a click
from being blocked. The addresses are resolved by OPNsense's own MAC
alias and kept current; the block rules are registered by the plugin
and never added to the owner's own rules.

Two devices behind the same switch still reach each other; blocking
those needs the switch.

Plugin Changelog
================

1.0.3

* automatic isolation, off until turned on: a device os-netreport 1.0.7
  records reaching a command-and-control list that os-blocklists blocks
  is isolated by its MAC address for the time set (1 to 720 hours, 24 by
  default) - the addresses it is seen with blocked both ways and their
  open connections cut, one it takes later blocked within a minute - and
  shown with its reason, its time and an Undo button
* never isolated: the firewall itself and its gateways, its HA peer, an
  address logged in to its web GUI or SSH, and an address the page was
  opened from in the last 7 days
* let go when its time ends, by Undo, or when the option is turned off,
  and why is kept and shown

1.0.2

* an update installed only as its owner signed it: the release's
  manifest checked with the release key this plugin carries, and the
  archive's SHA-256 and size the signed ones
* the latest release's signature read by the tag its manifest names,
  and a manifest too deep to parse refused by its signature

1.0.1

* a device listed loses the connections it already has open: cut at Apply and every minute
* configctl portguard apply does the whole job: the alias, the filter, the aliases, the cut
* the table emptied when no device is left; the page counts devices, and only what a rule blocks
* the Block button shows why a device was refused
* the rules hook is checked by php -l, before the installer copies it
* removal stops its cut, deletes its state and empties its table
* the page's menu entry carries an icon in the XGS section, and a Font Awesome 6 one in its own

